WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Hey guys, I’m Ola.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.760
I built Clause for Sandstone.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:04.640
Let’s jump in.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.040
Here’s the workspace.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.400
I can see what’s waiting on legal, open a request, and get to the document from one place.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.600
Down in the corner, it tells me what I’m running on: five documents, and SQLite.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.250
Let me add a request.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:19.370
It’s fictional.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:25.290
Acme wants a one year disclosure term, while keeping the exceptions and five year survival period.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.610
We also need to update the governing law.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.930
I write the outcome on the card, so I can keep checking what we’re trying to achieve.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.050
I’ll open the agreement from the request.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:40.050
The five sample contracts are read only, so I make my own copy before I touch anything.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:45.170
Then I can start where the work actually is: finding the clause I need to change.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:49.370
I’m searching for the two year disclosure term, as exact text.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:53.050
The result gives me the matching passage and the document version.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.410
When I open it, Clause selects that exact text.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:00.810
I don’t have to hunt through the agreement or guess which occurrence the result meant.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.730
Now I’ll replace two years with one year.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:07.570
I’m staging the edit first, so the saved document hasn’t changed.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.890
The engine checks that this target exists and identifies one place.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:17.090
If the wording repeats, I need context or an occurrence number to make it precise.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.530
Here’s the redline.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:22.250
Red is what I’m removing, and green is what I’m adding.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.690
We’re still on version one.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.810
Every edit in a batch refers to that same original text, so an earlier replacement can’t move a later target.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.250
If one edit is invalid, none of the batch saves.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.420
Now, Clause CI is a check against requirements I’ve saved for this document.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.100
The copy came with a few.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:47.740
I’ll add two exact ones: keep the survival clause word for word, and require the five year period.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.820
Exact checks compare literal text, so they don’t need a model call.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.260
Let me deliberately make a bad change.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.420
I’m staging a replacement of the two year term with a perpetual period.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.580
The edit itself is possible.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.180
But a perpetual term is something we said we wanted to avoid.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.780
Nothing has been saved yet, and that’s the important part.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.700
When I run the check, the no perpetual term rule fails against the proposed text.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:15.820
It’s an exact check, with no model involved.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:20.460
The model-judged requirements get their own label, and I treat those as advice.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.780
An edit can be precise and still break the thing I wanted to preserve.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.780
I’ll discard that mistaken change, stage the one year change again, and check again.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.060
Now the no perpetual term rule holds.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.020
Exact checks are repeatable checks on text.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:41.380
When I ask a model to judge meaning, I treat its answer as advice that can be wrong.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.820
That looks right, so I’ll apply it.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:45.660
We now have version two.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.660
The saved text, search index, and history update together.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.700
I still decide whether the legal request is complete.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.100
Applying an edit doesn’t automatically mean the business outcome has been met.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.340
Next, I’ll use the assistant for the governing law change.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.860
I’m asking for Delaware to become New York while keeping those exceptions and the survival period.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.340
It’s the same fictional agreement, and I’m asking for a proposal I can inspect.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.350
This part uses a real model request.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.870
The model suggests edits; it can’t save them directly.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:19.830
The usual editing engine checks the targets afterward.

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:23.830
I’ve bounded the request to twenty five seconds and limited the output.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.590
An oversized replacement gets rejected rather than cut off.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.350
Here’s the proposal, its explanation, and the version it was checked against.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.550
I can review it before I apply anything.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:41.710
Semantic checks also verify quoted evidence, but a real quote can still be used to support a wrong claim.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.630
I don’t treat that as legal approval.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:49.790
Now I’ll stage one more change, from shall give to may give, and run the check.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:55.190
The no perpetual term rule is an exact check, so it holds with no model involved.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.470
The notice rule is judged by a model, and it gets flagged.

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:03.030
Every result says how it was decided, and the model’s answers are advice.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:08.710
A model pass needs a quote that really is in the document, and a timeout is never a pass.

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:15.190
On sixty public NDAs, the model judge scored eighty percent, against forty for a keyword baseline.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.950
That’s a small experiment, not proof of legal reliability.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:22.990
I’ll discard that extra test edit and keep the governing law change.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.110
Then I apply it, and that creates version three.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.990
The server checks the version again at save time.

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:32.830
If someone else got there first, my stale edit would be rejected.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.710
It won’t silently overwrite their newer work.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.000
In Activity, I can see the revisions and the changes behind them.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.920
Restoring makes another version instead of erasing history.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.520
The Word export includes tracked changes, and a very large one runs as a background job.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.240
I tested accepting and rejecting those with independent readers; I’m not claiming every Word version was checked.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.560
Let’s search for the language I just saved.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.440
It’s available straight away.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:06.080
The server returns a consistent search snapshot, and the browser checks the loaded version and passage.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.920
If a hit is stale, it asks me to search again.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.440
It also protects any edits I already have staged.

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.130
I’ve also added source-backed templates.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:19.610
The settings show the source, version, license, and attribution.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:25.130
When I create a copy, I get an editable document, and its details keep that provenance.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:27.930
It’s a starting point that still needs review.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.170
These integrations are a demo with sample files.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.530
They show how browsing and importing a document would feel.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.610
They aren’t live customer repository connections.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:43.890
Real connections need the provider credentials and workspace admin setup, and I’ve kept that distinction visible.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.650
On mobile, search gets the full screen.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:52.370
That gives the passage and results room to breathe instead of squeezing them into a desktop dialog.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:06:01.450
The browser journeys cover mobile and desktop, including exact selection, protected drafts, and older responses arriving after a later navigation.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.460
Let me show you the API behind that edit.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:09.820
I send JSON with the version I expect, the text I’m targeting, and its replacement.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.100
The response gives me updated text and a change receipt.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.980
This calls the same editing service the screen uses.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.980
And here’s a real stale version response: a conflict.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:24.700
I asked to edit version one, but the document was already on version three.

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.740
The current text stays safe.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:30.500
Ambiguous targets and overlapping changes fail too.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:34.180
Offset edits must include the text expected at that position.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.140
The stack is fairly direct.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.100
React handles the interface.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.260
Fastify validates requests.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.020
A pure TypeScript engine resolves the changes.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:46.620
The database commits only if the version still matches.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.540
SQLite runs locally.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:55.020
The hosted app runs on Vercel with Supabase Postgres, and bodies over one mebibyte go to private Blob.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.820
Search indexes live in the database.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:09.550
This release passes four hundred and fifty eight SQLite tests, four hundred and forty nine Postgres tests, and twenty browser runs: ten journeys each on desktop and on a phone.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.990
The database suites overlap.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:16.110
CI also checks types, lint, and coverage floors.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:21.030
I measured a ten mebibyte document on local SQLite, on my laptop.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.390
One edit in the engine takes about seven milliseconds.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:30.470
Searching for words takes twenty one milliseconds for a rare word and twenty five for a common phrase.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:35.510
It first took eight point three seconds, because I built snippets for every matching document.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.630
Now I rank first, and snippet only the page.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:42.110
Under load, the results are mixed, and I’ve written them down.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:48.110
On one SQLite process, exact search and edits miss their targets with eight clients.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:08:00.910
On a laptop Postgres, after I rewrote the search chunk query, word search at the ninety fifth percentile fell from about sixteen hundred to about a hundred milliseconds, though the machine was loaded, so that’s diagnostic.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:07.390
The hosted run on October eighteenth, before that rewrite, missed its targets for metadata and word search.

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:10.110
No hosted run has used Supabase yet.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.000
Here’s what’s built and what’s proposed.

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:26.720
Built: Postgres on Supabase, bodies over one mebibyte in private Blob, search over eight thousand character chunks, and slow work as durable background jobs that return a two oh two with a status URL.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.040
Documents can go up to twenty five mebibytes.

00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.000
Proposed, not built: authentication, document permissions, and tenant isolation.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:42.200
I’d monitor latency by document size, conflicts, search delay, queue age, and model spend.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:48.760
Metrics go out over OpenTelemetry; I verified a local collector, but hosted delivery is untested.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.120
I’d test restoring backups and rolling back releases.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:58.120
The AI limits are per address, per hour today; real workspaces need durable budgets.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:00.250
A few limits to be clear about.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:06.410
The public demo is shared, so it’s for fictional data, and it has no sign-in or tenancy.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:11.530
Everything in this recording runs on a local seeded copy of the app, not the hosted site.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:15.610
Editing is plain text, and semantic review is advisory.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:20.570
An experimental evaluator called Jev is built behind a rollout gate and is off.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:23.650
The integrations page runs on sample files.

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:26.570
And I went beyond the suggested time box.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:31.730
That’s Clause: precise edits, searchable content, and a reviewable history.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:41.250
The repo has setup steps, sample requests, tests, measurements, and the one page infrastructure proposal, and it runs locally with a seeded database.

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:42.610
Thanks for taking a look.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.490
I’m happy to walk through the code and the trade-offs with you.
